# 🎮 Cross-Platform 2D Game Foundation (Android / iOS / Windows)

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Do this BEFORE writing any game logic.** Changing resolution strategy, camera setup, or UI scaling after development has started means rewriting layouts, repositioning objects, and debugging on every device again.

---

## 1. Choose a Reference Resolution

Pick **one resolution** as your design target. Everything you build will be authored at this resolution, then scaled to fit other devices.

| Reference Resolution | Aspect Ratio | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **1920 × 1080** | 16:9 | Most common across all platforms. Best starting point. |
| 1080 × 1920 | 9:16 | Only if your game is **portrait-only** (like a mobile card game). |

> [!TIP]
> **For a landscape 2D game, use `1920 × 1080` as your reference.**
> For a portrait 2D game, use `1080 × 1920`.

### Unity Setup
1. Go to **Game View** → click the aspect ratio dropdown → **+** → add `1920x1080`
2. Design your entire game at this resolution first

---

## 2. Camera Setup (The Most Critical Step)

For 2D games, the **Orthographic Camera** is your world ruler. Its `size` determines how many world units are visible vertically.

### The Formula

```
Orthographic Size = Reference Height / (2 × PPU)
```

- **PPU** (Pixels Per Unit) = how many pixels map to 1 Unity unit. Default is `100`.
- For 1080p: `Orthographic Size = 1080 / (2 × 100) = 5.4`

### Unity Setup
```
Main Camera → Projection: Orthographic
Main Camera → Size: 5.4
Main Camera → Background: Your background color
```

### What This Means
- The camera will always show **10.8 units vertically** (5.4 above + 5.4 below center)
- Horizontally, it shows `Screen Width / PPU` units — this **changes per device**
- On a 16:9 screen: `19.2 units wide`. On a 4:3 iPad: `14.4 units wide`

> [!CAUTION]
> **The horizontal extent changes across devices.** This is the #1 source of cross-platform bugs. You MUST handle this (see Section 4).

---

## 3. Pixels Per Unit (PPU) — Get This Right

PPU defines how your sprites map to the game world. **Set this once and never change it.**

| PPU | Best For | Sprite Size for 1 Unit |
|---|---|---|
| 100 (default) | Most 2D games | 100×100 px sprite = 1×1 unit |
| 32 | Pixel art games | 32×32 px sprite = 1×1 unit |
| 16 | Very chunky pixel art | 16×16 px sprite = 1×1 unit |

### Unity Setup
- Select your sprite → **Inspector** → **Pixels Per Unit**: set to your chosen value
- **All sprites must use the same PPU** for consistency

> [!TIP]
> If you're making a non-pixel-art 2D game, stick with **PPU = 100**. It's the Unity default and everything "just works."

---

## 4. Handling Different Aspect Ratios (THE BIG PROBLEM)

Real devices have wildly different aspect ratios:

| Device | Aspect Ratio | Difference from 16:9 |
|---|---|---|
| Most Android phones | 16:9, 18:9, 19.5:9, 20:9 | Taller/narrower |
| iPhone 15 | 19.5:9 | Much taller |
| iPad | 4:3 | Much wider |
| Windows PC | 16:9, 16:10, 21:9 | Wider |

### Strategy: **Lock Vertical, Adapt Horizontal**

This is the safest approach for most 2D games:

```
✅ Vertical height is ALWAYS the same (camera size is fixed)
✅ Wider screens see MORE of the world on the sides
✅ Narrower screens see LESS on the sides
```

### How to Implement

#### Option A: Expand the Visible Area (Recommended for most games)
Let the camera show more/less of the world horizontally. Place your **critical gameplay** in the center, and use **non-essential decoration** on the edges.

```
┌─────────────────────────────────┐  ← Ultra-wide (21:9)
│  decor  │   GAMEPLAY AREA   │  decor  │
│         │   (always visible) │         │
└─────────────────────────────────┘

    ┌───────────────────────┐  ← iPad (4:3)
    │   GAMEPLAY AREA       │
    │   (always visible)    │
    └───────────────────────┘
```

**Implementation Script — `CameraScaler.cs`:**
```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class CameraScaler : MonoBehaviour
{
    [Header("Reference Resolution")]
    public float referenceWidth = 1920f;
    public float referenceHeight = 1080f;
    public float referenceOrthographicSize = 5.4f;

    private Camera cam;

    void Awake()
    {
        cam = GetComponent<Camera>();
        AdjustCamera();
    }

    void AdjustCamera()
    {
        float referenceAspect = referenceWidth / referenceHeight;
        float currentAspect = (float)Screen.width / Screen.height;

        if (currentAspect < referenceAspect)
        {
            // Device is narrower than reference (e.g., tall phone)
            // Increase orthographic size to maintain width
            cam.orthographicSize = referenceOrthographicSize * 
                                   (referenceAspect / currentAspect);
        }
        else
        {
            // Device is wider than reference (e.g., iPad, ultrawide)
            // Keep the orthographic size as-is; more world is visible
            cam.orthographicSize = referenceOrthographicSize;
        }
    }
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> The script above ensures **all gameplay content is always visible**. On narrow screens, it zooms out slightly. On wide screens, you just see more.

#### Option B: Letterbox (Pillarbox)
Force a fixed aspect ratio and add black bars. Simpler but looks less polished.

```csharp
// Add black bars to enforce 16:9
float targetAspect = 16f / 9f;
float windowAspect = (float)Screen.width / Screen.height;
float scaleHeight = windowAspect / targetAspect;

if (scaleHeight < 1.0f)
{
    Rect rect = cam.rect;
    rect.width = 1.0f;
    rect.height = scaleHeight;
    rect.x = 0;
    rect.y = (1.0f - scaleHeight) / 2.0f;
    cam.rect = rect;
}
else
{
    float scaleWidth = 1.0f / scaleHeight;
    Rect rect = cam.rect;
    rect.width = scaleWidth;
    rect.height = 1.0f;
    rect.x = (1.0f - scaleWidth) / 2.0f;
    rect.y = 0;
    cam.rect = rect;
}
```

---

## 5. UI / Canvas Setup

UI (health bars, score, buttons, menus) must be handled **separately** from the game world.

### Canvas Scaler Settings

```
Canvas → Render Mode: Screen Space - Overlay
Canvas Scaler → UI Scale Mode: Scale With Screen Size
Canvas Scaler → Reference Resolution: 1920 x 1080
Canvas Scaler → Screen Match Mode: Match Width Or Height
Canvas Scaler → Match: 0.5 (balanced) or 1 (match height for landscape)
```

### Anchoring Rules (CRITICAL)

| UI Element | Anchor To | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Score text | Top-left or top-center | Stays at top on all screens |
| Health bar | Bottom-center | Stays at bottom |
| Pause button | Top-right | Stays in corner |
| Center popup | Center | Always centered |

```
Always use ANCHORS, never absolute positions for UI.
   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ Score          ⏸ Pause       │  ← Anchored to corners
   │                              │
   │                              │
   │        [Game World]          │
   │                              │
   │                              │
   │     ❤❤❤  Health Bar          │  ← Anchored to bottom
   └──────────────────────────────┘
```

> [!WARNING]
> **Never position UI elements using Transform (X, Y) values.** Always use **RectTransform anchors**. If you skip this, your UI WILL break on different screen sizes.

---

## 6. Safe Area (Mobile Notches & Rounded Corners)

Modern phones have notches, punch-holes, and rounded corners. Your UI must avoid these areas.

### Safe Area Script — `SafeAreaHandler.cs`

```csharp
using UnityEngine;

public class SafeAreaHandler : MonoBehaviour
{
    private RectTransform panelRect;
    private Rect lastSafeArea = Rect.zero;

    void Awake()
    {
        panelRect = GetComponent<RectTransform>();
        ApplySafeArea();
    }

    void ApplySafeArea()
    {
        Rect safeArea = Screen.safeArea;
        if (safeArea == lastSafeArea) return;
        lastSafeArea = safeArea;

        Vector2 anchorMin = safeArea.position;
        Vector2 anchorMax = safeArea.position + safeArea.size;

        anchorMin.x /= Screen.width;
        anchorMin.y /= Screen.height;
        anchorMax.x /= Screen.width;
        anchorMax.y /= Screen.height;

        panelRect.anchorMin = anchorMin;
        panelRect.anchorMax = anchorMax;
    }
}
```

### How to Use
1. Create a **Panel** under your Canvas called `SafeAreaPanel`
2. Attach `SafeAreaHandler.cs` to it
3. Put **ALL your UI elements inside this panel**
4. The panel auto-shrinks to avoid notches/rounded corners

---

## 7. Input Abstraction

You need ONE input system that works across touch, keyboard, mouse, and gamepad.

### Use Unity's New Input System

```
Window → Package Manager → Unity Registry → Input System → Install
```

### Why
| Platform | Input Method |
|---|---|
| Android/iOS | Touch |
| Windows | Keyboard + Mouse + Gamepad |
| All | Unity Input System handles all via Action Maps |

### Setup Steps
1. Create an **Input Action Asset** (right-click → Create → Input Actions)
2. Define **Action Maps**: `Player`, `UI`
3. Define **Actions**: `Move`, `Jump`, `Pause`
4. Add **Bindings** for each action:
   - `Move` → Keyboard WASD + Gamepad Left Stick + Touch Joystick
   - `Jump` → Keyboard Space + Gamepad South Button + Touch Tap

> [!TIP]
> **Never use `Input.GetKey()` or `Input.GetAxis()` directly.** These only work with keyboard/gamepad and make adding touch support painful later.

---

## 8. Project Settings Checklist

### Player Settings (Edit → Project Settings → Player)

| Setting | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Default Orientation | Landscape Left | Or your chosen orientation |
| Auto-rotate | Enable only your supported orientations | Prevents weird rotations |
| Default Resolution (Windows) | 1920 × 1080 | Matches your reference |
| Allow Fullscreen | Yes | Windows |
| Target Frame Rate | 60 | `Application.targetFrameRate = 60;` |

### Quality Settings
| Setting | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| VSync | Don't Sync (mobile) | Use `targetFrameRate` instead |
| Anti-aliasing | 2x or off (mobile) | Performance |
| Texture Quality | Full Res | Don't downscale sprites |

### Graphics
- Use **URP (Universal Render Pipeline)** — it's optimized for all platforms
- If starting a new project, choose the **2D (URP)** template

---

## 9. Folder Structure (Set Up Now, Thank Yourself Later)

```
Assets/
├── _Project/                  ← All your game content
│   ├── Art/
│   │   ├── Sprites/
│   │   ├── Animations/
│   │   └── UI/
│   ├── Audio/
│   │   ├── Music/
│   │   └── SFX/
│   ├── Prefabs/
│   │   ├── Player/
│   │   ├── Enemies/
│   │   └── UI/
│   ├── Scenes/
│   │   ├── MainMenu.unity
│   │   ├── Gameplay.unity
│   │   └── Loading.unity
│   ├── Scripts/
│   │   ├── Core/              ← Camera, Input, GameManager
│   │   ├── Player/
│   │   ├── Enemies/
│   │   ├── UI/
│   │   └── Utils/
│   ├── Input/                 ← Input Action Assets
│   └── Settings/              ← Render pipeline, quality profiles
├── Plugins/                   ← Third-party SDKs
└── StreamingAssets/           ← Platform-specific data
```

---

## 10. The Exact First Steps (Do This In Order)

```mermaid
graph TD
    A["1. Create Unity Project<br/>(2D URP Template)"] --> B["2. Set Reference Resolution<br/>(1920×1080 in Game View)"]
    B --> C["3. Configure Camera<br/>(Ortho, Size=5.4, add CameraScaler.cs)"]
    C --> D["4. Set Up Canvas<br/>(Scale With Screen Size, 1920×1080 ref)"]
    D --> E["5. Add Safe Area Panel<br/>(SafeAreaHandler.cs)"]
    E --> F["6. Install New Input System<br/>(Create Action Maps)"]
    F --> G["7. Create Folder Structure"]
    G --> H["8. Configure Player Settings<br/>(Orientation, Resolution, FPS)"]
    H --> I["9. Test on Multiple Resolutions<br/>(16:9, 4:3, 19.5:9, 21:9)"]
    I --> J["10. NOW Start Building Your Game ✅"]
```

---

## Quick Test Checklist

Before writing any game code, verify your setup by adding a simple colored square and UI text, then test these resolutions in the **Game View**:

| Test Resolution | Aspect | What to Check |
|---|---|---|
| 1920 × 1080 | 16:9 | Everything looks as designed |
| 2560 × 1440 | 16:9 | Still looks correct |
| 1080 × 1920 | 9:16 | (Skip if landscape-only) |
| 2048 × 1536 | 4:3 | iPad — UI still anchored correctly |
| 2532 × 1170 | 19.5:9 | iPhone — no clipping |
| 2560 × 1080 | 21:9 | Ultrawide — no gaps |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> If something breaks at any of these resolutions, **fix it now**. It only gets harder to fix later.

---

## Summary: What You're Protecting Against

| Problem | Solution |
|---|---|
| Sprites getting cut off on narrow phones | `CameraScaler.cs` adapts the view |
| UI overlapping notches | `SafeAreaHandler.cs` |
| UI breaking on different screens | Canvas Scaler + Anchors |
| Touch not working on mobile | New Input System |
| Inconsistent sprite sizes | Fixed PPU across all sprites |
| Spaghetti project later | Organized folder structure from day 1 |
